# Multi-env opt-in: copy to environments.tfvars (gitignored) and re-apply
# bootstrap locally —
#   terraform -chdir=terraform/bootstrap apply -var-file=environments.tfvars
# then create each GitHub Environment and feed it that env's role ARN
# (docs/bootstrap.md "Adding an environment"). Solo-account mode needs none
# of this: the variable defaults to dev only.
#
# This map is the ONLY place the environment list is enumerated. Everything
# downstream is derived: the bounded CI role, the boundary policy (including
# each env's sibling denies), the per-env budget, the state-key wall — and,
# outside Terraform, promote.yml/destroy.yml take the env as a plain string
# and validate it against terraform/envs/<env>/. Adding env #4 is an entry
# here plus its env root and values file; no workflow is edited.
#
# trust_main_ref stays FALSE for every gated env: dev alone is deployed
# straight from main by ci.yml, and a gated env whose role also trusted the
# bare main ref could be applied by a job that never passed its approval.
#
# Each RUNNING env is its own VPC + NAT + EKS control plane + nodes + ALB +
# ElastiCache. budget_usd is a tripwire, not a cap — size it for the posture
# you intend (an ephemeral promote-drill env is not a month of uptime).
environments = {
  dev     = { budget_usd = 25, trust_main_ref = true }
  staging = { budget_usd = 15 }
  prod    = { budget_usd = 15 }
}
